.TH UART 3
.SH NAME
eia0 \- the serial port, raw
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bind #c /dev
.nf

.B /dev/eia0
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Eia0
is the serial port's bytes before the console's line discipline sees them.
It is one of the files the
.B #c
device serves.
See
.IR cons (3)
for the rest of the directory.
.PP
While nothing has
.B eia0
open, every byte off the wire goes to the console's line discipline, the
same as a byte typed at the local keyboard.
The first open of
.B eia0
diverts the port: from that point the line discipline hears nothing from
it, and every byte goes to this file instead, until the last fid holding
it closes.
A byte that arrived while the file was diverted and nobody read it does
not queue for the console afterwards.
The ring is drained both when a fid opens the file and when the last one
closes it.
.PP
A read parks until a byte is available, unless one already is.
Nothing here is a line discipline: a read answers exactly the bytes that
arrived, in order, and none of them are echoed or interpreted.
There is no end of file: a read answers bytes or it parks, and it never
answers zero bytes.
.PP
A write puts its bytes on the wire and nowhere else: unlike
.BR /dev/cons ,
a write to
.B eia0
draws no glyph on the screen.
It always takes the whole write, because the hardware behind it has no
back pressure to report, only a queue it waits for room in.
.PP
Opening
.B eia0
on a machine with no serial port, or one that failed its self-test at
boot, fails with
.BR ENXIO .
.SH SOURCE
.B kernel/devfs/tap.odin
.br
.B kernel/drivers/uart
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (3),
.IR cons (3),
.IR kbd (3),
.IR eiafs (4)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Opening
.B eia0
with no port present fails with
.BR ENXIO .
A read that is still unanswered when the connection abandons it fails with
.BR EINTR .
